  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA surface low continues to move east, away from the area and \nout to sea. An inverted trough will extend to the west of the\nlow and will be the focus of some isolated shower activity that\nwill move from north to south through the afternoon into the\nearly evening. RAP forecasts suggest that we could see upwards\nof 500 to 1000 J/kg MUCAPE this afternoon but with very weak\nshear. Thus a few rumbles of thunder may be possible, but the\nthreat for strong convection appears low. This activity will\ndiminish with the loss of daytime heating, giving way to only a\nfew clouds overnight. Overnight valley and coastal fog seems\nlikely but density and coverage remains uncertain. For now, will\nbroad brush patchy fog over the valleys and along the entire\ncoast in the 06z to 13z timeframe tonight into Saturday morning.\nForecast lows will mainly be in the 50s. \n\nSurface high pressure and ridging aloft will lead to a mostly\ndry Saturday but a few more isolated showers will be possible in\nthe afternoon over the higher terrain. Temperatures will be a\nlittle bit warmer on Saturday than today with the ridge axis\nmoving overhead, but still around normal or slightly above\nnormal for this time of year. Highs are forecast to mainly range\nfrom the mid 70s to mid 80s. Clouds will be on the increase late\nin the day Saturday and overnight as we transition back into\nsouthwest flow aloft and the next trough approaches from the\nwest. Saturday night lows will then be a bit more mild than\ntonight, expected to range from the upper 50s to the mid 60s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA trough will be located across the Great Lakes Sunday morning,\nwith increasing SW flow into New England. Will expect \nincreasing cloudiness for the day as moisture increases from the\nwest. Can't rule out the development of some afternoon showers \nacross the mountains, but low level moisture and the increasing \ncloudiness limiting instability should keep these isolated to \nscattered. \n\nInto Monday, cluster analysis shows different flavors of the \n500mb trough amplitude and timing in the global ensembles. The \nfavored solution in the latest guidance, weighted mostly with \nthe GEPS and GEFS, is the least amplified. This leads to a drier\nsolution early Monday, but may allude to a later passage (rain \nchances continuing Monday night/Tuesday morning).\n\nWhile still fresh on our minds, there are a couple factors that\nare more impressive with this setup than the recent broad \nclosed low...but ultimately they are different animals. The \nfirst was slow moving and brought the threat for multi-day \nprecipitation, while this prospective event looks to occur in \nless than 24 hours. However, in that brief window the moisture \nceiling is higher. During the last event, IVT into northern New \nEngland was routed around the base of the incoming low before \nwrapping in from a SE direction off the Gulf of Maine towards \nthe coast. This event has a very direct SW injection along the \nEast Coast that is favorably channeled between low pres in the \nOhio Valley and high pressure over the Atlantic Ocean. This is \nnoted in likely GEFS and ENS IVT probabilities of exceeding 500 \nkg/m/s. ECMWF comparison to climo also indicates PWAT values \naround the 99th percentile along much of the Eastern Seaboard.\n\nThus, would expect excellent warm rain processes with efficient\nrainfall, but shorter duration. The general pattern supports \nthis return of moisture, but yet to be resolved is timing of \nthese ingredients. This can make the difference between taking \nadvantage of the complete system vs. only seeing a couple \ncomponents come in line before merging offshore. At this point, \neither the disconnect of ingredients or brevity prevents a \nnotable signal for excessive climo QPF based on the EFI.\n\nAfter this frontal system pushes through, the closed low may\nremain to the west advecting moisture through the Mid-Atlantic.\nAt this time, most guidance keeps deeper moisture south of our\narea, but there may be enough to provide the chance of showers\nthrough midweek amid a warming trend.",
